## Michael's Memo: Growth Capital, Structured Responsibly

Growing fast creates its own kind of capital problem, and it doesn't always look like trouble.

This month's client is a Colorado-based commercial excavation company that took over a decade to build its reputation. The current owners bought the business five years ago using an SBA 7(a) loan, the right tool at the time, though it came with a blanket lien that reached onto the owner's personal property, not just the business.

Since taking over, they've scaled the company from just over $5,000,000 in annual revenue to nearly $20,000,000 last year, with another 20 to 25% growth projected by year-end.

This year, they refinanced that SBA debt into a conventional bank facility, freeing the owner's personal property from the lien. A real win. But the new facility was sized to do one job: pay off the SBA balance. It didn't leave room for the capital a business growing this fast still needs to keep moving.

We've worked with this client for three years, since helping them untangle a stack of merchant cash advances that had gotten out of hand. They've grown steadily since, and this time they wanted a more responsible, accretive way to fund growth, knowing any new capital would have to sit behind their bank facility rather than in front of it.

We sourced several competitive options and structured a $650,000 subordinated term loan: a 20-month term, simple interest in the mid-teens, and a prepay discount that waives all remaining interest if paid off early, at any time. Closed within a couple of weeks.

If your growth, or a client's, has started to outpace what a bank facility alone can fund, we'd welcome the conversation.

Here to serve,

Michael Kodinsky, Founder & CEO

## Deal Highlight: $650K Subordinated Term Loan for a Commercial Excavating Company

**Client profile**

A Colorado-based commercial excavation company with more than a decade in business. Current ownership took over five years ago and has scaled revenue from just over $5,000,000 to nearly $20,000,000.

**Capital facility:** $650,000 subordinated term loan, 20-month term, simple interest in the mid-teens.

**The challenge**

The owners acquired the business five years ago using an SBA 7(a) loan, the right tool at the time, but one that placed a blanket lien across both the business and the owner's personal property. Since then, the company has nearly quadrupled its revenue and is projecting another 20 to 25% growth this year, the kind of scaling that requires real access to growth capital.

This year, the owners refinanced the SBA debt into a conventional bank facility, freeing the owner's personal property from the lien. But the new facility was sized only to retire the SBA balance. It left no room for the working capital the business needed to keep growing, and any additional funding would have to sit behind the new senior lender as subordinated debt.

**The solution**

Serve Funding sourced multiple competitive options and structured a $650,000 subordinated term loan behind the new bank facility: a 20-month term, simple interest in the mid-teens, and a prepay discount negotiated to waive all remaining interest on early payoff at any time, so it functions much like a standard line of credit. The deal closed within a couple of weeks.

{% callout type="tip" title="The takeaway" %}
Funding the next stage of growth took a different kind of capital, one built to sit responsibly behind a senior lender without slowing the business down. That's what the right capital, on the right terms, makes possible.
{% /callout %}

## More Than Money: Core Value of the Month, Responsibility

The "R" in T.R.U.S.T. is Responsibility, and this client came to Serve Funding seeking a more responsible, accretive way to fund growth, even though it meant a subordinated structure.

Responsibility meant sourcing real alternatives, respecting the new senior lender's position, and structuring a facility that actually fits how this business grows.

- We structure behind the bank, not around it.
- We source competitive options instead of defaulting to the easiest one.
- We explain the structure clearly, so everyone knows exactly what the client is signing.
- We never push a generic advance just to close faster.
